//BUG="a user macro is expanded inside the core library too"
//OUT="7"

// `insert_macros` (src/compiler/macros.rs) runs once over the tokens of the
// whole program — the user's file followed by src/core/*.cpl — with a single
// table of macros. A macro name is substituted wherever it appears as an
// identifier, so a macro in the user's file is also expanded inside the core
// library, in code the user cannot see.
//
// `res` is a local variable in `read_i64`, `read_str` and `_Str_from_raw`.
// With the macro below, those become `7 = 0L`, `7 = Str()` and so on, built
// from tokens that carry positions in the user's file but sit in the middle
// of core code. The parser merges the positions of neighbouring tokens,
// `FilePosition::add` (src/compiler/error.rs) asserts that both come from
// the same file, and the compiler dies:
//
//     assertion `left == right` failed: cannot join positions in different files
//
// Which names are affected is whatever the core library happens to use
// today: `res`, `c`, `i`, `s`, `x`, `a`, `b`, `vec`, `size`, `iter`, `next`,
// `push`, `start`, `end`, … `macro size { 100 }` does not panic but fails
// with "Unexpected token" pointing at the macro's own body, because
// `fn size` in src/core/vector.cpl has become `fn 100`.
//
// 10_macros/09 records that a macro shadows a variable of the same name in
// the user's own code. This is different: the program below never uses
// `res` for anything else.
//
// The fix is to expand each file with the macros that are visible to it —
// the core library must not see the user's.

macro res {
    7
}

fn main
    x = res
    out "{x}"
